# =============================================================================
# sap-adt - environment-variable credential layer
#
# Copy this file to a private location, fill it in, and DECLARE ITS PATH:
#   cp .env.example ~/.config/sap-adt.env && chmod 600 ~/.config/sap-adt.env
#   export SAP_ADT_DOTENV="$HOME/.config/sap-adt.env"
#
# The CLI never scans the current directory, the skill directory or a
# guessed install path for a ".env"; only the file named by SAP_ADT_DOTENV
# is read. Leave SAP_ADT_DOTENV unset to use profiles only.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOADING LOGIC
#
# Credential lookup order (first complete layer wins):
#   1. Real process environment variables (export SAP_URL= ...)
#   2. The dotenv file declared by SAP_ADT_DOTENV
#   3. ~/.sap-adt-cli/config.json profile + OS keystore (env -> keyring ->
#      dpapi/WSL2 -> pass -> encrypted file)
#
# Per-key precedence when layers 1 and 2 overlap: the real shell environment
# variable always wins over the value in .env. The two layers are merged
# per key, so the four REQUIRED values may be split between the shell and
# .env.
#
# ALL-OR-NOTHING:
#   The four REQUIRED variables (SAP_URL, SAP_USERNAME, SAP_PASSWORD,
#   SAP_CLIENT) must ALL resolve from the shell + .env combined. If even one
#   is missing, this whole layer is ignored and the CLI falls back to
#   config.json profiles - values already present here are NOT merged
#   per-field into a profile (no "URL from .env, password from keystore").
#
#   When the four required variables are complete they override EVERY
#   profile; --profile / SAP_PROFILE is then ignored for connection data.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTES / GOTCHAS
#
# - The password here is PLAINTEXT on disk (no OS keystore). Keep chmod 600
#   and never commit it. The repository .gitignore already excludes .env and
#   .env.* while tracking this .env.example.
# - Write/activate/transport operations still ask for a one-time [y/N]
#   confirmation per operation even when SAP_ALLOW_WRITE=1.
# - SAP_ENVIRONMENT=prd hard-refuses writes and transports regardless of
#   SAP_ALLOW_WRITE / SAP_ALLOW_TRANSPORT. Valid values: dev, qas, prd.
# - Boolean values accept: 1, true, yes, y, on (case-insensitive).
# - SAP_VERIFY_SSL is enabled by default; ONLY the exact value "0" disables
#   it (use 0 for self-signed/internal-CA SAP systems).
# - This is a minimal hand-written parser: plain KEY=VALUE lines only.
#   No "export " prefix, no inline ("#") comments, no variable expansion.
#   Comments must start the line with #. Surrounding single/double quotes
#   around the value are stripped.
# - Keystore variables SAP_ADT_<PROFILE>_PASSWORD and
#   SAP_ADT_MASTER_PASSPHRASE belong to PROFILE mode (layer 3) and are read
#   from the real process environment only. Defining them in this .env file
#   has NO effect; export them in the shell instead (see bottom of file).
# =============================================================================

# --- Required (all four must be present for this layer to activate) ---------
SAP_URL=https://your-sap-system.example.com:8000
SAP_USERNAME=YOUR_USERNAME
SAP_PASSWORD=YOUR_PASSWORD
SAP_CLIENT=100

# --- Optional connection settings -------------------------------------------
# SAP_LANGUAGE=EN
# SSL verification: set to 0 only for self-signed / internal-CA endpoints.
# SAP_VERIFY_SSL=1

# --- Environment label & capability switches --------------------------------
# dev (default when unset) | qas | prd (prd forces both switches below off).
# SAP_ENVIRONMENT=dev
# Enable write/activate commands (per-operation [y/N] confirmation remains).
# SAP_ALLOW_WRITE=0
# Enable transport-release commands.
# SAP_ALLOW_TRANSPORT=0

# =============================================================================
# EXAMPLE: complete read/write DEV system (self-signed certificate)
#
# SAP_URL=https://dev-sap.internal:8000
# SAP_USERNAME=DEVELOPER
# SAP_PASSWORD=your-dev-password
# SAP_CLIENT=100
# SAP_LANGUAGE=EN
# SAP_VERIFY_SSL=0
# SAP_ENVIRONMENT=dev
# SAP_ALLOW_WRITE=1
# SAP_ALLOW_TRANSPORT=0
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXAMPLE: split between shell and .env (secret stays out of the file)
#
# Export in the shell first:
#   export SAP_PASSWORD='your-password'
# then put only the non-secret values in .env:
#   SAP_URL=https://qas-sap.internal:8000
#   SAP_USERNAME=DEVELOPER
#   SAP_CLIENT=200
#   SAP_ENVIRONMENT=qas
# The four required keys are complete across the two layers, so profiles are
# skipped; the shell value wins for SAP_PASSWORD.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROFILE-MODE variables (layer 3) - shell environment ONLY, never in .env:
#
# Per-profile password for the read-only 'env' keystore backend, where
# <PROFILE> is the config.json profile name uppercased (profile qas-1 ->
# SAP_ADT_QAS-1_PASSWORD):
#   export SAP_ADT_DEV_PASSWORD='your-dev-password'
# Master passphrase for the encrypted-file keystore in non-interactive runs:
#   export SAP_ADT_MASTER_PASSPHRASE='your-master-passphrase'
# Select a config.json profile (only consulted when the four required SAP_*
# connection variables are NOT all present):
#   export SAP_PROFILE=dev
# =============================================================================
